// of the CMake target.

//! \defgroup core_api Core API
//! \brief Everything you need to control a Robotiq gripper.
//!
//! The gripper SDK is composed of the Gripper class and free functions that
//! operate on Gripper objects.
//!
//! \verbatim
//!               ConnectionConfig                Serial + Platform + Logger
//!             (SerialConfig + slave addr)     (custom transport, RTOS port,
//!                     |                             or a test double)
//!                     |                                 |
//!                     |    (built-in serial)            |    (bring your own serial)
//!                     +----------------+----------------+
//!                                      v
//!                             +-----------------+
//!                             |     Gripper     |   <- the class you drive
//!                             +-----------------+
//!                            /         |         \
//!                 setCommand()   getStatus()   connectionState()
//!                       /             |             \
//!                      v              v              v
//!             GripperCommand   GripperStatus   ConnectionState
//!               (you send)     (FaultStatus
//!                                lives inside)
//! \endverbatim
//!
//! \note all gripper methods are not represented in this graph. Check gripper
//! class documentation for details.
//!
//! While Gripper class methods like setCommand and getStatus are enough
//! to control the gripper, high level functions are provided to
//! ease some actions like activation and fault recovery. Those functions are
//! not members of the gripper class and directly use gripper object
//! to perform an action.
//!
//! See \ref testing "Testing & CI Utilities" for driving this same API
//! without a physical gripper attached.

//! \defgroup commanding Commands & Status
//! \ingroup core_api
//! \brief The typed blocks a Gripper exchanges with the hardware: the
//! command you write to it, and the status you read back from it.

//! \defgroup command Command
//! \ingroup commanding
//! \brief What you send to the gripper to perform an action.

//! \defgroup action ActionByte
//! \ingroup command
//! \brief The packed action-request byte (byte 0 of the command block).

//! \defgroup status Status
//! \ingroup commanding
//! \brief The information you receive to understand gripper
//! status.

//! \defgroup gripper_status Gripper Status
//! \ingroup status
//! \brief The packed GRIPPER STATUS byte (byte 0 of the status block).

//! \defgroup fault_status Fault Status
//! \ingroup status
//! \brief The packed FAULT STATUS byte (byte 2 of the status block).

//! \defgroup activation Activation & Recovery
//! \ingroup core_api
//! \brief The blocking activation / fault-recovery procedures built on
//! top of Command and Status, and the polling helpers they are built from.

//! \defgroup connection Connection & Configuration
//! \ingroup core_api
//! \brief How a Gripper is configured, and the state of its connection.

//! \defgroup runtime Runtime & Extension Points
//! \ingroup core_api
//! \brief The injectable transport, platform, and logging interfaces
//! Gripper is built on — implement these to add a custom transport, port
//! to an RTOS, or capture logs in tests.
//! Most users do not need to implement these interfaces.
//!
//! Split by what you're implementing, plus what a failure in any of them
//! looks like to the caller:
//!   - **Transport** — the byte-level link to the hardware (Serial).
//!   - **Platform** — the OS services the threaded runtime needs
//!     (locks, threads, sleeps).
//!   - **Logging** — where diagnostic lines go.
//!   - **Exceptions** — the driver- and transport-level failures thrown
//!     up through these interfaces.
//!   - **Modbus Client** — the no-thread layer below Gripper, for the
//!     rare integration that cannot run Gripper at all.

//! \defgroup transport Transport
//! \ingroup runtime
//! \brief The byte-level transport extension point (Serial).

//! \defgroup platform Platform
//! \ingroup runtime
//! \brief The OS services (locks, threads, sleeps) behind the threaded runtime.

//! \defgroup logging Logging
//! \ingroup runtime
//! \brief Where diagnostic log lines go.

//! \defgroup exceptions Exceptions
//! \ingroup runtime
//! \brief Failures thrown by the transport or driver.

//! \defgroup modbus_client Modbus Client
//! \ingroup runtime
//! \brief The no-thread layer below Gripper: one Modbus transaction per
//! call, for superloop targets and diagnostic tooling only.

//! \defgroup testing Testing & CI Utilities
//! \brief Tools for exercising the SDK without a physical gripper attached.
//!
//! Not part of the real control path — see \ref core_api "Core API" for that.
